---
title: Lines and Areas
description: Choose and compose line, range-area, rolling-statistic, and annotation patterns for ordered data.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lines answer how a value changes across an ordered domain. Areas add a second\nmeaning: distance from a baseline or the span between two boundaries. Use that\nextra area only when the reader should compare magnitude, accumulation, or an\ninterval—not merely because a filled chart looks stronger."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the comparison"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does one measure change over time?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One line with an explicit temporal scale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do several measures change together?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Several lines with direct labels or a legend"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where does one measure exceed another?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A difference mark with distinct positive and negative fills"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is the local trend after reducing short-term noise?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A raw line plus a clearly named rolling statistic"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What range surrounds a central estimate?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An area with explicit lower and upper channels"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which observations deserve explanation?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A line plus selected text, dots, rules, or bands"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does composition change over time?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A stacked or normalized area in "},{"type":"link","href":"./stacked-and-composition.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stacked and Composed Charts"}]}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The x domain must have a meaningful order. Do not connect nominal categories\njust because they appear in an array."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-with-one-ordered-series","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with one ordered series"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use one line when the first task is reading change over a shared sequence.\nPoints keep the individual observations available for focus and tooltips."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"basic-line-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"basic-line","data-collapsed-indexes":"[]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\\n\\nconst rows = [\\n  { month: 'Jan', downloads: 42 },\\n  { month: 'Feb', downloads: 58 },\\n  { month: 'Mar', downloads: 51 },\\n  { month: 'Apr', downloads: 73 },\\n  { month: 'May', downloads: 81 },\\n]\\n\\nconst chart = defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    lineY(rows, {\\n      x: 'month',\\n      y: 'downloads',\\n      points: true,\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      lineCap: 'butt',\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      nice: true,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Downloads (thousands)' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\\n\\nexport default chart\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', downloads: 42 },\n  { month: 'Feb', downloads: 58 },\n  { month: 'Mar', downloads: 51 },\n  { month: 'Apr', downloads: 73 },\n  { month: 'May', downloads: 81 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'downloads',\n      points: true,\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      lineCap: 'butt',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Downloads (thousands)' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart","lang":"ts","meta":"group=basic-line env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineCap: 'butt'"},{"type":"text","value":" ends the stroke at each path endpoint. Omit it to retain the\ndefault round endpoints. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineX"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineCap"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineJoin"},{"type":"text","value":"\noptions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-several-series-from-a-common-baseline","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare several series from a common baseline"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Indexing each series to its first observation compares relative change when the\noriginal magnitudes are not directly comparable. Direct end labels reduce the\nwork of matching line colors to a separate legend."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"55-indexed-multi-line","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prepare the indexed values in the application, state the baseline, and retain\nthe original values for exact-value disclosure. Use a stable series channel so\ncolor, path grouping, focus, and updates agree on identity.\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/data-and-channels.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data and Channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers series grouping,\nwhile "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/legends-and-color.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legends and Color"}]},{"type":"text","value":" explains when a\nseparate legend is the better choice."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"show-a-derived-trend-honestly","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show a derived trend honestly"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A moving average is a derived series, not a visual curve setting. Prepare the\nrolling values with the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rollingWindow"},{"type":"text","value":" transform beside the definition, keep\nthe original time domain, and name the rolling window in surrounding text or a legend."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"19-moving-average-line","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changing interpolation only changes the path between observations. It does not\nperform smoothing or create evidence between samples. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/transforms.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data Transforms"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for rolling windows and reducers."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-two-boundaries-at-their-exact-crossings","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare two boundaries at their exact crossings"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A difference chart separates the intervals where a primary series is above or\nbelow a comparison. Keep any rolling statistic or forecast calculation in data\npreparation, then give both boundaries to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"differenceY"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"differenceY(rows, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y1: 'average',\n  y2: 'Close',\n  positiveFill: '#16a34a',\n  negativeFill: '#dc2626',\n  comparisonStroke: '#475569',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mark finds each sign change, interpolates the exact crossing, and owns the\npositive area, negative area, comparison line, and primary line. Application\ncode does not need to prepare sign runs or duplicate boundary rows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"../overview.md#a-chart-is-a-composition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overview"}]},{"type":"text","value":" shows the complete\nrendered Apple closing-price example. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/difference.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Difference Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for transposed\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"differenceX"},{"type":"text","value":", gap behavior, fill suppression, lineage, and interaction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-context-with-an-interval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add context with an interval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Bollinger band combines a rolling center line with an interval derived from\nlocal variation. The band is context for the observed series; it is not a\nconfidence interval unless the underlying calculation actually defines one."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"22-bollinger-band","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compute the rolling statistics once and share those rows between the interval\nand center line:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const bands = rollingWindow(aapl, {\n  size: 20,\n  orderBy: 'Date',\n  anchor: 'end',\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: {\n    meanClose: { value: 'Close', reduce: 'mean' },\n    closeDeviation: { value: 'Close', reduce: deviation },\n  },\n})\n\nareaY(bands, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y1: (row) => row.meanClose - row.closeDeviation * 2,\n  y2: (row) => row.meanClose + row.closeDeviation * 2,\n})\n\nlineY(bands, { x: 'Date', y: 'meanClose' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rolling-window length, estimator, and multiplier are authored statistical meaning.\nThe transform owns ordering and source lineage; the area owns interval\ngeometry. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/line-and-area.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Line and Area Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for\nthe channel contracts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"annotate-selected-observations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Annotate selected observations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Annotations should explain a small number of meaningful points. Selecting the\nminimum and maximum in data preparation makes the intent auditable and avoids\nplacing a text label on every observation."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"58-select-extrema","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer dots and text over the same scales rather than baking labels into a line\nrenderer. "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/marks-and-layering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks and Layering"}]},{"type":"text","value":" explains why\nseparate layers remain easier to update and extend."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve missing values when a gap is meaningful. The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../quick-start.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":" demonstrates an explicit line gap."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a temporal scale for dates and define the domain in application data\nsemantics, as described in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve row IDs or unique positions across updates, and group series with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":"; supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" only when the mark cannot infer identity. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/dynamic-data-and-animation.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Data and Animation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let automatic layout measure tick labels, then verify the smallest container\nin "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/responsive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use position, labels, or line treatment in addition to color when a\ndistinction is essential. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/accessibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-comparison","text":"Choose the comparison","level":2},{"id":"start-with-one-ordered-series","text":"Start with one ordered series","level":2},{"id":"compare-several-series-from-a-common-baseline","text":"Compare several series from a common baseline","level":2},{"id":"show-a-derived-trend-honestly","text":"Show a derived trend honestly","level":2},{"id":"compare-two-boundaries-at-their-exact-crossings","text":"Compare two boundaries at their exact crossings","level":2},{"id":"add-context-with-an-interval","text":"Add context with an interval","level":2},{"id":"annotate-selected-observations","text":"Annotate selected observations","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nLines answer how a value changes across an ordered domain. Areas add a second\nmeaning: distance from a baseline or the span between two boundaries. Use that\nextra area only when the reader should compare magnitude, accumulation, or an\ninterval—not merely because a filled chart looks stronger.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                          | Start with                                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| How does one measure change over time?                   | One line with an explicit temporal scale                                                    |\n| How do several measures change together?                 | Several lines with direct labels or a legend                                                |\n| Where does one measure exceed another?                   | A difference mark with distinct positive and negative fills                                 |\n| What is the local trend after reducing short-term noise? | A raw line plus a clearly named rolling statistic                                           |\n| What range surrounds a central estimate?                 | An area with explicit lower and upper channels                                              |\n| Which observations deserve explanation?                  | A line plus selected text, dots, rules, or bands                                            |\n| How does composition change over time?                   | A stacked or normalized area in [Stacked and Composed Charts](./stacked-and-composition.md) |\n\nThe x domain must have a meaningful order. Do not connect nominal categories\njust because they appear in an array.\n\n## Start with one ordered series\n\nUse one line when the first task is reading change over a shared sequence.\nPoints keep the individual observations available for focus and tooltips.\n\n```ts group=basic-line env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', downloads: 42 },\n  { month: 'Feb', downloads: 58 },\n  { month: 'Mar', downloads: 51 },\n  { month: 'Apr', downloads: 73 },\n  { month: 'May', downloads: 81 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'downloads',\n      points: true,\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      lineCap: 'butt',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Downloads (thousands)' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n`lineCap: 'butt'` ends the stroke at each path endpoint. Omit it to retain the\ndefault round endpoints. `lineX` accepts the same `lineCap` and `lineJoin`\noptions.\n\n## Compare several series from a common baseline\n\nIndexing each series to its first observation compares relative change when the\noriginal magnitudes are not directly comparable. Direct end labels reduce the\nwork of matching line colors to a separate legend.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=55-indexed-multi-line height=480 -->\n\nPrepare the indexed values in the application, state the baseline, and retain\nthe original values for exact-value disclosure. Use a stable series channel so\ncolor, path grouping, focus, and updates agree on identity.\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) covers series grouping,\nwhile [Legends and Color](../guides/legends-and-color.md) explains when a\nseparate legend is the better choice.\n\n## Show a derived trend honestly\n\nA moving average is a derived series, not a visual curve setting. Prepare the\nrolling values with the public `rollingWindow` transform beside the definition, keep\nthe original time domain, and name the rolling window in surrounding text or a legend.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=19-moving-average-line height=480 -->\n\nChanging interpolation only changes the path between observations. It does not\nperform smoothing or create evidence between samples. See\n[Data Transforms](../reference/transforms.md) for rolling windows and reducers.\n\n## Compare two boundaries at their exact crossings\n\nA difference chart separates the intervals where a primary series is above or\nbelow a comparison. Keep any rolling statistic or forecast calculation in data\npreparation, then give both boundaries to `differenceY`:\n\n```ts\ndifferenceY(rows, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y1: 'average',\n  y2: 'Close',\n  positiveFill: '#16a34a',\n  negativeFill: '#dc2626',\n  comparisonStroke: '#475569',\n})\n```\n\nThe mark finds each sign change, interpolates the exact crossing, and owns the\npositive area, negative area, comparison line, and primary line. Application\ncode does not need to prepare sign runs or duplicate boundary rows.\n\nThe [Overview](../overview.md#a-chart-is-a-composition) shows the complete\nrendered Apple closing-price example. See\n[Difference Marks](../reference/marks/difference.md) for transposed\n`differenceX`, gap behavior, fill suppression, lineage, and interaction.\n\n## Add context with an interval\n\nA Bollinger band combines a rolling center line with an interval derived from\nlocal variation. The band is context for the observed series; it is not a\nconfidence interval unless the underlying calculation actually defines one.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=22-bollinger-band height=480 -->\n\nCompute the rolling statistics once and share those rows between the interval\nand center line:\n\n```ts\nconst bands = rollingWindow(aapl, {\n  size: 20,\n  orderBy: 'Date',\n  anchor: 'end',\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: {\n    meanClose: { value: 'Close', reduce: 'mean' },\n    closeDeviation: { value: 'Close', reduce: deviation },\n  },\n})\n\nareaY(bands, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y1: (row) => row.meanClose - row.closeDeviation * 2,\n  y2: (row) => row.meanClose + row.closeDeviation * 2,\n})\n\nlineY(bands, { x: 'Date', y: 'meanClose' })\n```\n\nThe rolling-window length, estimator, and multiplier are authored statistical meaning.\nThe transform owns ordering and source lineage; the area owns interval\ngeometry. See [Line and Area Marks](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md) for\nthe channel contracts.\n\n## Annotate selected observations\n\nAnnotations should explain a small number of meaningful points. Selecting the\nminimum and maximum in data preparation makes the intent auditable and avoids\nplacing a text label on every observation.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=58-select-extrema height=480 -->\n\nLayer dots and text over the same scales rather than baking labels into a line\nrenderer. [Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md) explains why\nseparate layers remain easier to update and extend.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Preserve missing values when a gap is meaningful. The\n  [Quick Start](../quick-start.md) demonstrates an explicit line gap.\n- Use a temporal scale for dates and define the domain in application data\n  semantics, as described in\n  [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n- Preserve row IDs or unique positions across updates, and group series with\n  `z`; supply `key` only when the mark cannot infer identity. See\n  [Dynamic Data and Animation](../guides/dynamic-data-and-animation.md).\n- Let automatic layout measure tick labels, then verify the smallest container\n  in [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n- Use position, labels, or line treatment in addition to color when a\n  distinction is essential. See [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Lines and Areas\ndescription: Choose and compose line, range-area, rolling-statistic, and annotation patterns for ordered data.\n---\n\nLines answer how a value changes across an ordered domain. Areas add a second\nmeaning: distance from a baseline or the span between two boundaries. Use that\nextra area only when the reader should compare magnitude, accumulation, or an\ninterval—not merely because a filled chart looks stronger.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                          | Start with                                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| How does one measure change over time?                   | One line with an explicit temporal scale                                                    |\n| How do several measures change together?                 | Several lines with direct labels or a legend                                                |\n| Where does one measure exceed another?                   | A difference mark with distinct positive and negative fills                                 |\n| What is the local trend after reducing short-term noise? | A raw line plus a clearly named rolling statistic                                           |\n| What range surrounds a central estimate?                 | An area with explicit lower and upper channels                                              |\n| Which observations deserve explanation?                  | A line plus selected text, dots, rules, or bands                                            |\n| How does composition change over time?                   | A stacked or normalized area in [Stacked and Composed Charts](./stacked-and-composition.md) |\n\nThe x domain must have a meaningful order. Do not connect nominal categories\njust because they appear in an array.\n\n## Start with one ordered series\n\nUse one line when the first task is reading change over a shared sequence.\nPoints keep the individual observations available for focus and tooltips.\n\n```ts group=basic-line env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', downloads: 42 },\n  { month: 'Feb', downloads: 58 },\n  { month: 'Mar', downloads: 51 },\n  { month: 'Apr', downloads: 73 },\n  { month: 'May', downloads: 81 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'downloads',\n      points: true,\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      lineCap: 'butt',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Downloads (thousands)' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n`lineCap: 'butt'` ends the stroke at each path endpoint. Omit it to retain the\ndefault round endpoints. `lineX` accepts the same `lineCap` and `lineJoin`\noptions.\n\n## Compare several series from a common baseline\n\nIndexing each series to its first observation compares relative change when the\noriginal magnitudes are not directly comparable. Direct end labels reduce the\nwork of matching line colors to a separate legend.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=55-indexed-multi-line height=480 -->\n\nPrepare the indexed values in the application, state the baseline, and retain\nthe original values for exact-value disclosure. Use a stable series channel so\ncolor, path grouping, focus, and updates agree on identity.\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) covers series grouping,\nwhile [Legends and Color](../guides/legends-and-color.md) explains when a\nseparate legend is the better choice.\n\n## Show a derived trend honestly\n\nA moving average is a derived series, not a visual curve setting. Prepare the\nrolling values with the public `rollingWindow` transform beside the definition, keep\nthe original time domain, and name the rolling window in surrounding text or a legend.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=19-moving-average-line height=480 -->\n\nChanging interpolation only changes the path between observations. It does not\nperform smoothing or create evidence between samples. See\n[Data Transforms](../reference/transforms.md) for rolling windows and reducers.\n\n## Compare two boundaries at their exact crossings\n\nA difference chart separates the intervals where a primary series is above or\nbelow a comparison. Keep any rolling statistic or forecast calculation in data\npreparation, then give both boundaries to `differenceY`:\n\n```ts\ndifferenceY(rows, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y1: 'average',\n  y2: 'Close',\n  positiveFill: '#16a34a',\n  negativeFill: '#dc2626',\n  comparisonStroke: '#475569',\n})\n```\n\nThe mark finds each sign change, interpolates the exact crossing, and owns the\npositive area, negative area, comparison line, and primary line. Application\ncode does not need to prepare sign runs or duplicate boundary rows.\n\nThe [Overview](../overview.md#a-chart-is-a-composition) shows the complete\nrendered Apple closing-price example. See\n[Difference Marks](../reference/marks/difference.md) for transposed\n`differenceX`, gap behavior, fill suppression, lineage, and interaction.\n\n## Add context with an interval\n\nA Bollinger band combines a rolling center line with an interval derived from\nlocal variation. The band is context for the observed series; it is not a\nconfidence interval unless the underlying calculation actually defines one.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=22-bollinger-band height=480 -->\n\nCompute the rolling statistics once and share those rows between the interval\nand center line:\n\n```ts\nconst bands = rollingWindow(aapl, {\n  size: 20,\n  orderBy: 'Date',\n  anchor: 'end',\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: {\n    meanClose: { value: 'Close', reduce: 'mean' },\n    closeDeviation: { value: 'Close', reduce: deviation },\n  },\n})\n\nareaY(bands, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y1: (row) => row.meanClose - row.closeDeviation * 2,\n  y2: (row) => row.meanClose + row.closeDeviation * 2,\n})\n\nlineY(bands, { x: 'Date', y: 'meanClose' })\n```\n\nThe rolling-window length, estimator, and multiplier are authored statistical meaning.\nThe transform owns ordering and source lineage; the area owns interval\ngeometry. See [Line and Area Marks](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md) for\nthe channel contracts.\n\n## Annotate selected observations\n\nAnnotations should explain a small number of meaningful points. Selecting the\nminimum and maximum in data preparation makes the intent auditable and avoids\nplacing a text label on every observation.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=58-select-extrema height=480 -->\n\nLayer dots and text over the same scales rather than baking labels into a line\nrenderer. [Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md) explains why\nseparate layers remain easier to update and extend.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Preserve missing values when a gap is meaningful. The\n  [Quick Start](../quick-start.md) demonstrates an explicit line gap.\n- Use a temporal scale for dates and define the domain in application data\n  semantics, as described in\n  [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n- Preserve row IDs or unique positions across updates, and group series with\n  `z`; supply `key` only when the mark cannot infer identity. See\n  [Dynamic Data and Animation](../guides/dynamic-data-and-animation.md).\n- Let automatic layout measure tick labels, then verify the smallest container\n  in [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n- Use position, labels, or line treatment in addition to color when a\n  distinction is essential. See [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n"}
